  {
    "episode_id": 78165,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/8/78165.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 14,
    "turn_text": "Well, it's kind of an unconventional situation in that the You have an economy which a lot of people thought was going into recession. And in fact, it's definitely not. I mean, the last quarterly report had the economy growing at almost 3% annual rate, which would be a pretty dynamic move. And the employment numbers are good, except in certain industries. You've got the media industry. You've got the digital industry cutting a lot of jobs. And so there are all these mixed signals going on. And I don't even want to begin to talk about the stock market and how that reflects things, because a lot of times you have good news in the economy and the stock market, as it did today, turned bad. And yesterday, because they're afraid the Fed will then say, OK, we need to keep raising interest rates at a high rate. And that's bad for stocks, because if you can make more money, from a dividend or from a treasury note, then you don't take as much risk with with equities. So it's just a very confusing economic situation. If I were giving investment advice, which I would be low to",
