  {
    "episode_id": 78165,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/8/78165.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 16,
    "turn_text": "I would just say, sit tight and wait till this thing sifts out. Because I think What I've kept saying over the last few years is, when economists have tried to read things into what's going on, particularly with inflation, we're dealing with unprecedented changes in the economy because of the pandemic. So you had one year where prices dropped dramatically because basically the economy a lot of it shut down and you had all these other things going on like supply chain issues and so forth and then you had the war in Ukraine and gas prices spike and all these things which are not normal economic cycles there and so yeah you get huge inflation numbers but a lot of that was because prices had gone down and they and then when they go up the percentages get higher not to say that this did not have and has not had an impact on people's lives because obviously food prices, gas prices did go up. But they, they, they didn't go up from normal. A lot of it was not normal supply and demand issues. It was, you know, the price of eggs went up because the avian flu hit chickens and 5 million chickens had to be killed. So there are all these crazy things going on. And It just has been a very unusual economic cycle. So that's why you get these some strong economic numbers and yet people in some industries, they're looking at a bleak picture again in the digital world with, it's not Facebook anymore, mega platforms. And Amazon and a",
